            <ArticleTitle>Recurrent pain in college students.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>67-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The authors investigated the prevalence of recurrent pain in a college student sample. Of the 1,564 students surveyed, 467 (29%) indicated that they had experienced recurrent pain at an intensity that was mostly in the mild-to-moderate range. Students who reported having recurrent pain were significantly older and more depressed than students who did not indicate they suffered from recurrent pain. Pain intensity was positively correlated (r = .14) with depression among the students who reported having recurrent pain. Implications of the findings for future research, treatment, and health promotion efforts are discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
